OUTSTANDING FROM CODE REVIEW
----------------------------
These are accepted for now because this is a dummy/test site.
They MUST be dealt with before any real user data exists.

[AUTH]  — DEFERRED BY DECISION (2026-09-29), not overlooked
  Items 3 and 4 are a real authentication bypass: anyone can mint a superuser
  session with no password. They are knowingly left in place because proper
  logins are not needed yet and there is other work queued first.

  Treat these as the gate for going beyond a dummy site, not as outstanding
  bugs. The moment this site holds anything real, or is reachable by anyone
  who is not you, items 3 and 4 are the first thing to fix — before any other
  item in this file.

3) Remove the hardcoded 2FA bypass in the login flow.
   backend/server.js:271-274 — `else if (code === '000000') { verified = true; }`
   Combined with two_factor_secret defaulting to NULL for every account, this
   lets anyone mint a superuser JWT with no password.
   Also make login-step1 issue a single-use challenge token that login-step2
   must present; right now step 2 trusts the username in the request body.

4) Decide the fate of POST /api/auth/login (backend/routes/auth.js:9-83).
   It is a second login door that skips 2FA completely and is what the
   "sign in as another user" modal uses (frontend/src/superuser.js:40).
   Either gate it through the same 2FA flow or delete it.
   Also: no rate limiting anywhere — 6-digit TOTP with `window: 1` and
   unlimited attempts is brute-forceable.

[ASSET WORKFLOW]

5) Creative approval cannot approve a NEW repository asset.
   backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js:~1980 — the INSERT has 3 columns but
   4 placeholders:
       INSERT INTO assets (name, asset_type, description) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)
   better-sqlite3 throws "4 values for 3 columns", and the surrounding catch
   turns it into a misleading 409. (Feature is still in progress.)

6) The approval workflow is still API-only above the read layer.
   LARGELY ADDRESSED — see PHASE 1 COMPLETE and FILE ACCESS above.

   Done: the admin review queue (GET /creative-approvals); GET /assets returns
   creative_locked / repository_version_id / latest_check / latest_approval;
   the developer panel renders all of it; and the files themselves can now be
   read — the manifest endpoint and the per-file content endpoint. An approver
   is no longer blind.

   What remains:
     - no UI to request an approval, approve or reject. The endpoints exist and
       are discoverable, but all three actions still require calling the API by
       hand.
     - no client. The manifest is designed to be consumed by something that
       syncs a working copy by hash — a small CLI that reads the manifest and
       fetches only the files whose sha256 differs locally. Nothing does that
       yet, so today it is manual.
     - no archive download. A single zip is a convenience for someone without a
       working copy; deliberately not built yet (see docs/assettypes01 layer B
       for why a manifest-and-per-file shape was chosen first).
     - GAME-SPACE READ — DEFERRED BY DECISION, bundled with building the game.
       See the dedicated entry below; it is deliberately not being built yet.

7) Set assets.recommended_version_id (or drop the columns).
   `assets.recommended_version_id`, `asset_recommended_versions`,
   `asset_comments`, `sessions`, `api_keys` and `audit_logs` are all created in
   backend/db.js but never written or read. audit_logs is the notable gap for an
   approval workflow that should record who approved what.

[SECURITY / HARDENING]

8) Stored XSS in the invite table.
   frontend/src/sharedInvites.js:84-105, 169, 178 interpolate invite.email /
   invite.role into innerHTML. POST /api/inviteDevs/generate-invite
   (backend/routes/inviteDevs.js:41) only checks `if (!email)`, so an invite
   to `<img src=x onerror=...>` executes in the admin session.
   frontend/src/developerWorkspace.js already does this correctly with
   escapeHtml — copy that approach.

9) Add upload limits.
   backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js:196-207 — multer is configured with
   only `maxCount: 1000`: no `limits.fileSize`, no `fileFilter`, and no
   client-side size check. Any developer account can fill the disk via
   backend/uploads/tmp. Also add a client-side MAX_TOTAL_SIZE guard and, if
   progress feedback is wanted, switch the upload to XMLHttpRequest.

10) Real session revocation.
    Logout now clears the cookie, but tokens are stateless, so a copied token
    survives until it expires. The `sessions` table in db.js is fully defined
    and has never had a row written to it — nothing writes it, nothing reads it.
    It could hold a jti per login, checked in authenticateToken.

    This is now BLOCKING something visible: item 28 wants an admin panel showing
    who is logged in, and there is no record of a login anywhere to show. One
    piece of work — record a session per login, check it on the way in —
    delivers revocation, "who is logged in", and per-device logout together.

11) Add CSRF protection.
    Cookie flags are now consistent (httpOnly, sameSite=lax, secure in
    production), but there is still no CSRF token.

12) Stop returning err.message verbatim to clients.
    backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js ~:1018 and ~:2179 leak
    SQLite/filesystem internals.

13) OAuth token is passed in a URL query string.
    backend/server.js:143 — `/portal.html?token=<jwt>` leaks via referrer,
    browser history and server logs. Use a short-lived one-time code instead.

14) Treat dashboard role checks as cosmetic.
    frontend/src/header.js:6-33, admin.js, superuser.js:174 gate on
    localStorage.user_info. The /api routes are properly guarded, but the
    dashboard shells are served by express.static with no auth
    (backend/server.js:54). admin.js additionally performs no role check at all.

[CLEANUP]

15) Fix backend/reset/resetAssetData.js:~7.
    `storageDir = path.join(__dirname, '../../uploads/files')` resolves to
    <repo>/uploads/files, but files live in <repo>/backend/uploads/files.
    The script therefore wipes the stored_files table and then reports
    "nothing to delete", orphaning every physical file permanently.

16) Use '' instead of NULL for root-level relative_path.
    backend/db.js:178-182 — UNIQUE(workspace_version_id, relative_path,
    filename) does not constrain root files because SQLite treats NULLs as
    distinct. Only the in-process `logicalPaths` set prevents duplicates today.

17) Drop or fix the filename_overrides feature.
    backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js:~434 looks the override up by the
    already-edited final path, while frontend/src/developerWorkspace.js:572
    keys it by the original path — the lookup can never hit, so the feature is
    dead code.

18) Missing GET endpoints for developer workspace storage.

19) Dead code / minor frontend fixes.
    - frontend/src/superuser.js:20-27 (`signInAsBtn` exists in no HTML)
    - frontend/src/admin.js:21 (element id `invites-table-body` is wrong;
      the real id is `invite-list-body`) and admin.js never renders stats
    - cloudflareControl.html has no JS handlers bound (unlike repoControl.html)
    - frontend/src/sandboxEngine.js:64-74 splices inside forEach, skipping
      collectibles
    - frontend/src/developerWorkspace.js:649-655 wipes the success message it
      just set, because clearUpload() clears #uploadStatus
    - frontend/src/sharedInvites.js:23-42 — reissue calls a non-existent
      route, and the expiry badge uses createdAt instead of the API's
      expiresAt; `new Date('2026-09-29 14:00:00')` is Invalid Date in Safari

20) Repo hygiene — remaining tracked junk.

    STILL TRACKED, STILL WRONG:
      * logiNNotes.txt — contains a bcrypt hash.
      * assets/images/mud01.nk — an 11MB Nuke script.

    CORRECTED 2026-10-02: this item claimed "~40 editor backup files (*~,
    #file#) are tracked". They are not, and cannot be — git ls-files matches no
    file whose name contains a tilde or a hash. That line had been carried
    forward without being rechecked, which is how every stale claim in this file
    starts.

    The backups do still exist ON DISK though — 36 of them, through
    frontend/src/components and docs. Harmless to git, but they are the exact
    class of file the upload route now refuses, and they will keep appearing for
    as long as an editor writes them.

21) Guard req.body destructuring (Express 5 behaviour change).
    Express 5 sets req.body to undefined when no body parser matched, where
    Express 4 gave {}. So any unguarded `const { x } = req.body` throws a
    TypeError and returns a 500 instead of a clean 400 when a client posts
    without a JSON content-type. Affected, all outside a try block:

        backend/routes/auth.js:10           (username, password)
        backend/routes/superuser.js         (github/keys, cloudflare/keys)
        backend/routes/inviteDevs.js:42     (email, role)
        backend/server.js                   login-step1, login-step2,
                                            create-invite, register-invited

    Fix is mechanical: `const { x } = req.body ?? {};`, or use `req.body?.x`.
    backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js already does this correctly.
    Low severity (malformed requests only) but trivially avoided.

22) [DONE 2026-10-01] npm audit is CLEAN — 0 vulnerabilities.

    nodemailer 9.0.5 -> 10.0.13, done with the email work rather than before
    it, as planned: bumping a major version with no call sites would have meant
    validating it against nothing. The mail path is now exercised by tests
    against a stub relay, so the bump was checked against real use.

    Worth recording a mistake made on the way: the first attempt installed
    `nodemailer@^7`, which DOWNGRADED from 9.0.5 and left the advisory in
    place. `npm audit` still reported 1 high severity, which is what caught it.
    The fix was 10.x.


23) REGRESSION GUARD: keep the fallbacks last, and test it.
    backend/server.js section 8 holds the /api JSON 404 and the SPA catch-all.
    Express matches in registration order, so a catch-all registered too early
    silently swallows every route declared below it. That is not theoretical --
    it is exactly how login-step1, login-step2, the Google OAuth routes,
    create-invite, register-invited and setup-2fa all became unreachable and
    login broke with a "Not found" body.

    Nothing else in the test suite catches this, so there is now a check:

        npm run check:routes      # scripts/check-route-order.js

    It stubs listen(), walks app.router.stack and fails if any route or
    mounted router sits after the catch-all. Verified in both directions: it
    reports all 8 shadowed handlers against 50baa00 and passes on the current
    tree. Run it before committing any change to server.js.

24) Known-missing DOM ids, listed by npm run check:dom.
    Real gaps, not typos. Each is exempted in scripts/check-dom-ids.js with its
    reason, so a NEW missing id still fails the check while these stay visible.

      runDiagnosticsBtn  (superuser.js)
        Bound to POST /api/superuser/developer/diagnostics, which works. The
        button lived in developerInviteExp.html — a component only ever built
        into the DEVELOPER dashboard — so it was never on the superuser page
        that binds it. Needs adding to the superuser panel.

      signInAsBtn  (superuser.js)
        The entry point for impersonation. Deferred by decision: impersonation
        is how an admin reaches inside a developer workspace, and it is the
        ONLY way in — there is deliberately no admin read path.

      invite-link-input  (sharedInvites.js)
        copyInviteLink() selects it, but nothing renders a generated link with
        that id, so the copy helper does nothing. Belongs with the sharedInvites
        rework above.

    Remove an entry when the control is added or the code is deleted.

25) DEVELOPER PAGE: browse the game store, to reuse approved assets.
    A developer should be able to browse what is already in the game store —
    particularly IMAGES and MODELS — and pull it into their own work, rather
    than re-uploading a copy of something the store already holds.

    This is the consumption side of the model, and it is the same ground as
    GAME-SPACE READ below: neither exists, because both were deferred until
    the game itself is being built. Recorded separately because it is a
    DEVELOPER-facing requirement with its own shape — browsing by type,
    previewing an image or a model, and importing into a workspace — whereas
    game-space read is the access layer underneath it.

    What it needs, when the time comes:

      * a /api/game router, and the THIRD access class: workspace content is
        the owning developer only, game-store content is readable by any
        authenticated developer, release management is admin/superuser.
      * import into a workspace, writing workspace_assets.repository_asset_id
        and repository_base_version_id — both exist in the schema and neither
        has ever been written. That is what would finally make "pick up your
        own approved work and continue" possible.
      * preview for the types that can be previewed: images inline (with the
        usual care — octet-stream, never the stored mime), and models via a
        glTF viewer. See docs/assettypes01 for why only some formats are
        renderable.

    Note the quota interaction: content pulled from the game store is
    EXCLUDED from the workspace limit, because it is not what the workspace is
    staging. That is already implemented, so importing approved work is free —
    which is the point.

    --- THE OTHER HALF: TELL THEM ON THE WAY IN ---

    NOT IMPLEMENTED, and it is the sentence this item opens with. The lookup
    exists — shaInGameStore in routes/developerWorkspace.js — but it is used in
    exactly ONE place, the quota projection, and only to avoid charging for
    content the store already holds. It never refuses and never says anything.

    SO TODAY: a developer can upload content that is already in the game store,
    and nothing tells them. Note what that does and does NOT do — there is ONE
    AND ONLY ONE COPY of any SHA-256 in the project, enforced by
    UNIQUE (sha256) on stored_files, so this writes NO new bytes to disk. The
    existing row is reused and the developer's version simply references it.
    Nothing is duplicated and no quota is charged.

    The cost is not storage, then. It is that the developer silently does
    something pointless and is never offered the better path — pulling the
    approved copy — which is what this item exists to provide.

    WHAT IT SHOULD DO: detect it and say so, naming the asset it already exists
    as. Then the developer pulls the approved copy instead of keeping a second
    one — which is the whole point of this item, and the reason the check
    belongs with the import path rather than bolted on separately.

    --- DECIDED 2026-10-02: REFUSE THE TRANSFER, POINT AT THE EXISTING FILE ---

    If a file's content is ALREADY IN THE GAME STORE — same sha256 — the upload
    is REFUSED and the developer is TOLD WHY. What they get instead is a
    reference: their own file name, pointing at the stored file that already
    exists.

    So the bytes are never transferred and never stored twice. The developer
    keeps their name for it, the store keeps its one copy, and the developer is
    told what happened rather than the system silently doing something clever.

    WHY REFUSE RATHER THAN ACCEPT QUIETLY, which is what happens today: the
    current behaviour already reuses the stored file, but says nothing. The
    developer sees a successful upload and has no idea the content they just
    sent is already approved — so they carry on iterating on a copy of
    something the game already has. Refusing costs them one round trip and
    tells them the thing worth knowing.

    A NOTE ON THE WORDING, because this is a refusal that still produces a
    result — the file entry exists afterwards, pointing at the existing
    content. It is the TRANSFER that is refused, not the operation. If that is
    the wrong reading, it is the message and the linking step that change, not
    the detection.

    THIS SETTLES THE EARLIER OPEN QUESTION. Warn-or-refuse is answered: refuse.
    The near-match prompt remains the pattern for genuinely ambiguous cases;
    this is not one, because a matching SHA is not a judgement call.

    STILL TO SETTLE WHEN BUILDING: what "already in the store" means when only
    SOME of an upload's files match. Per-file it is unambiguous — this file's
    bytes are already approved. Per-asset it is not, and the message must not
    claim more than the evidence: "these 3 files are already in the store" is
    supportable, "this asset already exists" may not be.

26) LEAK PREVENTION — stopping a build escaping into the wild.

    Put on the list as a topic, not yet a task. No build distribution exists
    yet, so there is nothing to protect; but the DESIGN of distribution should
    account for this from the start rather than being retrofitted.

    THE PREMISE: it cannot be prevented. Anyone with a copy can leak it. Every
    real technique is deterrence, attribution or damage limitation.

    Attribution is the highest-value one for a studio this size — knowing WHO
    leaked is worth more than slowing anyone down. Spec 01 already says
    "Dynamic account telemetry is embedded in client binaries for leak
    tracing", which is this. Practical forms: per-user binary edits (different
    constants, reordered functions) or steganographic marks in assets/shaders.
    A visible username overlay is worth little — it is cropped in seconds.

    Also already in spec 01, and worth keeping when distribution is built:
      * HWID binding — "session tokens are tied to Hardware ID (HWID) hashes
        upon launch". Does not stop a determined attacker; kills casual
        sharing, which is most of it.
      * short-lived user-signed download URLs — limits the window in which a
        build can be grabbed.

    Revocation ALREADY WORKS and needs nothing built: authenticateToken
    re-reads is_active on every request, so deactivating an account takes
    effect immediately rather than at token expiry. That covers both a
    developer and a tester.

    Staged content is the cheapest lever: do not put anything in an early
    build that could not afford to leak — no debug symbols, no source, no
    unreleased audio.

    DELIBERATELY NOT DRM. Denuvo-class protection is expensive, fragile,
    degrades the game, and delays rather than prevents. Wrong trade for
    pre-release builds shared with testers.

    AND THE PART THAT CANNOT BE ENGINEERED AWAY: developers have the source
    and assets on their machines because that is the job. Leak risk there is a
    trust and contract matter. Testers are the group the portal can actually
    control, which is why its access rules matter more for them.

27) DISCUSSION BOARD — SELF-HOSTED MATRIX / ELEMENT. Decided, not built.
    Full design note: docs/discussionboard01. Read that before starting.

    Requirements: open source, hosted in the UK or the EU, and no age-based
    message deletion. Slack fails on retention; Discord was chosen and then
    rejected on jurisdiction (US company, US servers, no EU residency, CLOUD
    Act exposure).

    WHAT WE WOULD RUN: Synapse + PostgreSQL + Element Web behind nginx, on a
    UK/EU machine. ~4 GB / 2 cores, EUR 5-10/month, no per-user fees.

    WHY SYNAPSE and not the lighter homeservers: Conduit's lineage is a mess
    (conduwuit discontinued 2025, work scattered across forks) and Dendrite is
    in maintenance mode. Decisively, the admin API and OIDC/SSO — the features
    the portal actually wants — exist only in Synapse. Cheap to run and unable
    to integrate is the wrong trade.

    THE POINT OF SELF-HOSTING IS INTEGRATION, not just jurisdiction. A
    third-party board means revoking portal access does NOT revoke board
    access. Self-hosting lets that be fixed: the portal's admin API calls can
    create a Matrix user on registration and DEACTIVATE it on deactivation, so
    a revoked tester loses the board the same moment they lose the portal.
    That is rung 2 of three in the note; rung 1 is the link-out that exists
    today, rung 3 is full SSO.

    THE DEPENDENCY IS NOW MET: account deactivation exists (admin panel,
    Accounts table; authenticateToken reads is_active and deactivating writes
    it, taking effect on the next request). So rung 2 has something to hook
    into when the board is built.

    WHAT EXISTS NOW, deliberately product-agnostic: frontend/discussion.html
    links out via VITE_DISCUSSION_URL and names no provider, so pointing it at
    the Element instance is one config line. Blank means the page says "not
    configured" rather than offering a dead link, which is the state today.

    STILL TO DO — all in the note's section 7 and 8: choose the machine (UK or
    EU, and it decides the whole jurisdiction argument), decide whether voice
    is needed (TURN and a lot of complexity go with it), decide room structure
    and whether roles mean anything on the board, and set retention explicitly
    since with self-hosting it is a config decision rather than a vendor's
    promise.

    Also: the manual's Discussion sections, which still describe a board we
    would have built rather than the one that exists. (They used to say this
    about the onboarding docs; those are gone and the manual carries the same
    text.)

    --- WHO CAN SEE THE BOARD, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS WHO CAN SIGN IN ---

    The board has its own membership and its own idea of which rooms you belong
    to. Being signed in to the portal does not mean being a member, and it
    certainly does not mean being a member of everything on it.

    A discussion block used to sit on the SIGN-IN FORM and the INVITATION PAGE —
    two pages seen ONLY by people who are not signed in — describing channels,
    direct messages and @mentions as though they were waiting inside. Removed
    from both. It promised the thing the portal cannot promise, to the one
    audience least able to check.

    IT IS STILL REACHABLE, and removing the links did not change that:

      * /discussion.html is served from frontend/dist like every other page, so
        a request for it gets a 200 with no session at all.
      * The board's URL is baked into the JavaScript bundle by VITE_ at build
        time, so when the board is configured the address will be readable by
        anyone who fetches the bundle — gating the page would not hide it.

    THE FIX IS NOT A PAGE GUARD. The URL has to come from an AUTHENTICATED
    ENDPOINT at runtime rather than being compiled into the bundle, and the page
    that displays it has to be served the way the manual is — outside
    frontend/dist, because express.static is mounted first and anything in that
    directory is fetchable by anyone whatever guards the route. Same reasoning
    as the manual, same shape of answer.

    --- AND DIRECT MESSAGES SHOULD BE VISIBLE WITHOUT GOING TO THE BOARD ---

    A direct message on the board should show at the TOP OF EVERY SIGNED-IN
    PAGE, as a note saying there are messages — so nobody has to remember to go
    and look. This is what the utility bar was built to have room for, and it is
    the first thing that belongs in it after the manual and the bin.

    BLOCKED ON THE BOARD EXISTING, and on the portal being able to ask it
    anything: this needs an API on the portal side that talks to the board, not
    a page that links to it. Do not build a placeholder that shows nothing —
    an indicator that is always empty is worse than no indicator, because
    people stop looking at it.


28) ADMIN AND SUPERUSER PAGES — to build. WHAT GOES ON THEM.

    The dashboards exist but are thin. What follows is the running list of
    things that either already exist with nothing calling them, or that the
    portal needs and has nowhere to show. Add to it rather than starting a new
    item.

    --- THE MENU NOW EXISTS AND NAMES ALL OF IT (2026-10-02) ---

    A menu was added to adminPanel.html, so it appears on BOTH the admin and the
    superuser dashboard — the same reason the accounts logic lives in a shared
    file. It carries every entry below that is a VIEW:

        Accounts  |  Invites  |  Reports  |  Sessions  |  Storage  |
        Health  |  Audit log

    THE TWO KINDS BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, ON PURPOSE. Accounts and Invites are
    BUILT, so they are plain anchors onto the panels already on the page — they
    scroll, and they do not pretend to be pages that do not exist. Everything
    else is a STUB: it reports itself as missing and names the TODO item it
    belongs to, via frontend/src/notBuilt.js, which wires any element carrying
    data-not-built.

    WHY STUBS RATHER THAN NOTHING. The shape of these pages is known and the work
    is recorded here, so the menu names it rather than the list living only in a
    document. And a menu entry that does nothing when clicked is indistinguishable
    from a bug — a stub tells you where to look instead of leaving you to guess.

    THEY ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TABS, and deliberately DO NOT switch panes. An
    empty pane reads as a broken page, which is worse than an honest "not built".

    DELETE THE STUB AS EACH FEATURE LANDS. When the last one goes,
    frontend/src/notBuilt.js goes with it.

    --- ALREADY BUILT, NOTHING CALLS IT ---

    ASSET CATEGORY RETAG. POST /assets/:assetId/category exists, is tested, and
    is admin/superuser only — but there is no admin view of workspace assets to
    hang a button on, so the only way to change a category today is a terminal.
    Give it a home; do not add a second endpoint.

    Read the ASSET CATEGORY block above for why it is a deliberate exception to
    the impersonation rule. That reasoning has to survive being put behind a
    button.

    BUG REPORTS — [DONE 2026-10-02]. A list with a status filter, each report
    showing its kind, who sent it and when, what they wrote, the page they were
    on as a link, and a reply address where one was left. Resolving takes an
    optional note, and the panel says when one is skipped. Anonymous reports
    are shown as information — "sent without signing in" — rather than as
    missing data.

    frontend/src/adminReports.html + adminReports.js, listed in build.js for
    BOTH dashboards the same way the accounts table is.

    --- NEEDED, AND BLOCKED ON SOMETHING ELSE ---

    WHO IS LOGGED IN. There is no data behind this today. Login is a stateless
    JWT in a cookie, so the server keeps no record of who holds a live token —
    the `sessions` table in db.js is fully defined and has NEVER HAD A ROW
    written to it by anything. Nothing writes it and nothing reads it.

    So this panel is not a UI job; it is blocked on item 10. Recording a
    session per login (and checking it in authenticateToken) is what makes
    "who is logged in", "log this device out" and real revocation possible —
    three features from one piece of work. Build item 10 first, then this is
    just a SELECT.

    Worth deciding when building: whether "logged in" means a live session row
    or "seen in the last N minutes". With a jti-per-login record those are the
    same question; without one, neither can be answered.

    --- NEEDED, NOTHING IN THE WAY ---

    DELETING FROM THE GAME ASSET STORE. Its own item — see 31 — because it is
    bigger than a control on a page: it needs a route, a confirmation that
    names what will be destroyed, and a decision about history. Listed here so
    whoever builds these pages does not build the panel without it and then
    find the asset management has nowhere to live.

    STORAGE — [DONE 2026-05-10]. The panel is built and shows both figures,
    side by side, with the note explaining why they do not reconcile. The two
    are now LIBRARIES rather than route-local code, because each is asked on
    more than one screen and the answers must not diverge:

      * backend/lib/workspaceUsage.js — what a developer is CHARGED.
      * backend/lib/hostStorage.js    — what the DISK holds.

    AND THE DEVELOPER'S NUMBER IS THE SAME FUNCTION. The admin tab does not sum
    the tables itself; it calls workspaceUsage() per workspace, the same call
    the developer's own panel makes. Two implementations would eventually
    disagree, and the disagreement would be invisible until somebody compared
    two screens — one developer being told they have room while the admin is
    told they do not. Same function, same answer, by construction.

    The status bands (ok / warning / critical) come from shared/usageStatus.mjs
    so the disk and a quota cannot mean different things by "nearly full", and
    the CLIENT IS NOT ASKED TO AGREE — it is sent a band and displays it.

    INODES ARE SHOWN WITH THE SPACE, which was not in the original plan and
    belongs there: a content-addressed store holds one file per SHA and game
    assets include a great many small ones, so the file table can run out while
    terabytes remain. It fails identically — every write refused — and a panel
    that showed only free space would call that disk healthy.

    STILL OPEN HERE:
      * The warning has to be VISIBLE WITHOUT GOING LOOKING, which this tab is
        not: it lives behind a tab. The banner belongs in the header or the
        utility bar, and that is the Health panel's job.
      * THRESHOLDS ARE HARDCODED at 85/95 in shared/usageStatus.mjs. Item 32
        wants them admin-editable through the settings mechanism, which does not
        exist yet. When it lands, the constants move behind it and the worker
        must READ the setting rather than cache it at boot.
      * The blob directory path '../uploads/files' is written out in FIVE
        places (this lib, jobs/daily.js, the upload route, two reset scripts).
        hostStorage.js exports BLOB_DIR; the other four should use it. Not done
        in this change because two of them are destructive reset scripts that
        would need re-testing to touch safely.

    The rest of this section is the reasoning that produced the above, kept
    because the accounting rules are easy to break later:

    PER DEVELOPER. The data exists and is already defined the way we
    agreed: workspaceUsage() sums a workspace's
    files against a quota, and workspaceStoredFileIds explicitly EXCLUDES
    anything that lives in the game store —

        AND wvf.stored_file_id NOT IN (SELECT stored_file_id FROM asset_version_files)

    So this number counts only what the developer owns: nothing shared with the
    game store counts against them, however many bytes it occupies on disk.
    Same rule on the way in — shaInGameStore() lets an upload of content the
    game already holds through without charging the quota. That is the decision,
    already implemented; the admin panel just needs to display it per developer
    and biggest-first.

    See WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE above for the full rule. One part of it
    is easy to get wrong later, so it is repeated here: a workspace is PRIVATE,
    and the game store is the only shared content in the system. Two developers
    uploading identical bytes get one copy on disk — content addressing cannot
    tell the same bytes from the same file — but that is deduplication, not
    sharing, and both are still charged, because each is staging their own copy.

    STORAGE, IN TOTAL — [DONE 2026-05-10, in backend/lib/hostStorage.js, which
    uses statfs: total, used, available and percent on the volume the blobs are
    actually on, plus the inode table. It reports bavail rather than bfree so
    the root reserve is not counted as room a process can use.]

    A DIFFERENT NUMBER, AND A DIFFERENT TOOL. The real
    question "are we about to run out of disk" is a HARDWARE question, not an
    accounting one, and the honest answer is df, not a sum of the tables. The
    per-developer figures above are what each developer is charged; they are not
    expected to add up to the disk, and they must not be presented as if they
    should. A file in the game store is charged to nobody and still occupies
    bytes. Show the filesystem: total, used, available, and percent — on the
    volume the store is actually on, whether that is a mounted disk, an object
    store, or somewhere else entirely.

    WARN WHEN IT IS GETTING FULL. Disk, and memory and CPU alongside it, with
    thresholds that warn on the admin page before anyone notices the hard way.
    This is the one panel whose job is to be boring until it is urgent, so an
    alert has to be visible without going looking for it.

    AND IT HAS TO BE READABLE BY CLAUDE. The admin needs to be able to discuss a
    problem and get help fixing it, which means the state of the system has to
    come out in a form that can be COPIED AND PASTED into a conversation —
    structured, complete, no truncation, stable field names, plain text or JSON.
    A screenshot is not this. Assume whoever pastes it is pasting it verbatim
    and by hand.

    THAT MAKES ONE REQUIREMENT ABSOLUTE: NO SECRETS IN IT. No password hashes,
    no tokens, no API keys, no SMTP credentials, no session cookies — nothing
    that would matter if it ended up in a chat log, a ticket, or a screenshot of
    a chat log. Redact by construction rather than by remembering: build the
    report from an explicit list of fields rather than by dumping a table and
    trimming it, because a dump grows a column the day someone adds one.

    --- ALREADY EXISTS, MAY WANT A VIEW ---

    The Accounts table (built), the invite list (built), GET /api/admin/stats
    (built: active/total users, pending invitations). The audit log is written
    by deactivation and by every category retag, feedback resolve, restore,
    purge and daily job run — and is readable by nothing at all. Worth a view
    for the same reason as the rest, and it is now the last of the
    "we already record this and nobody can see it" group.

    --- LAYOUT INCONSISTENCY — [SETTLED 2026-10-02]. The admin page is TABS,
    the same as the developer page.

    Frontend/src/adminTabs.js switches sections by id, so the panes do not have
    to be siblings in one .tab-content — which they cannot be, because these
    panels are separate components that build.js concatenates and no one of
    them can wrap the others. The nav lists EVERY section including the
    superuser-only ones, and an entry whose section is not on the page is
    removed, so the admin dashboard shows its tabs and the superuser dashboard
    shows its own from one file.

    The container oddity is also gone: devInvites.html no longer wraps itself
    in `<section class="container my-4">`; it is a plain section holding two
    cards, like the other panels.

    --- AND STILL ABSENT ON PURPOSE ---

    There is no admin read path into a developer's workspace. An admin who needs
    to see inside one does it by IMPERSONATION, which is marked, time-bounded
    and logged. Do not add a standing browse-anything view while building these
    pages, however convenient it looks next to the retag control.


29) BUG REPORT / FEEDBACK FORM — BUILT. Admins cannot read them yet.

    A form for reporting a fault or giving feedback, reachable from every page,
    signed in or not. The button is bottom-right on all eight pages.

    BUILT: shared/feedbackRules.mjs (the kinds and the limits, used by both
    sides), the feedback table, POST /api/feedback (open to everyone),
    GET /api/feedback and POST /api/feedback/:id/resolve (admin/superuser),
    optionalAuth in the auth middleware so an open route can still learn who is
    asking, and frontend/src/feedback.js — which BUILDS ITS OWN MARKUP, so a
    page gets the button from one script tag rather than a partial that has to
    be listed in build.js AND pasted into each hand-written page.

    VERIFIED, not assumed: anonymous reports store a null user; a signed-in
    report stores the actor from the token; and a body claiming
    user_id/username/role/created_at is IGNORED ENTIRELY — the row was recorded
    as the token's user with the server's clock, which is the whole point.

    NOT BUILT: no UI reads them. GET and resolve are API-only, which means
    reports currently go somewhere nobody looks. Same gap as item 28, and the
    same fix — they want building together.

    WHO AND WHEN ARE FILLED IN AUTOMATICALLY AND ON THE SERVER. The actor comes
    from the session token and the timestamp from the server clock. Neither is
    taken from the client: a client-supplied "who" is a claim, not a fact, and a
    client-supplied "when" is whatever the reporter's machine happens to think
    the time is. A report that cannot be trusted about who sent it is not worth
    collecting.

    WHAT THE CLIENT DOES supply is the one thing only it knows — the page and
    the URL the report is about. That is the "where", and without it a report
    costs a round trip to interpret. It is still untrusted input: truncated and
    escaped like any other.

    ANONYMOUS IS ALLOWED ON THE PUBLIC PAGES. Being unable to sign in is the
    single most likely thing to want to report, and by definition that person
    has no session. Those reports record no user, which is itself information —
    it says the report came from someone who could not get in.

    THREE OPEN QUESTIONS, AND ONE OF THEM WAS ANSWERED BY BUILDING IT:

    * KIND — SETTLED. A kind field was built: three options, Bug / Idea /
      Other, in shared/feedbackRules.mjs. Three and not ten because the point is
      to let someone triage a list at a glance, and past about three people
      start guessing; there is an explicit Other for anything that does not fit.
      UNLIKE the asset category, this one REJECTS an unknown value — the list is
      what makes the field worth having, and Other is always available, so being
      strict costs the reporter nothing. The two lists sit next to each other in
      shared/ and behave oppositely on purpose; both are commented.

    * A REPLY ADDRESS FOR ANONYMOUS REPORTS — SETTLED. Optional, shown only to
      someone with no session. Not required even then: making somebody hand over
      an address to report a login fault is a good way to get no reports.

    * WHO READS THEM — STILL OPEN, and it is item 28. The routes to list and
      resolve reports exist, are tested, and nothing calls them, so today they
      go somewhere nobody looks.


30) THE SHA STORE — audited 2026-10-02. One hole found, and it is not in the
    upload path.

    There is one and only one copy of any SHA-256 in the project, and the audit
    confirms the code holds that line. What follows is what was checked, so the
    next person does not have to re-derive it, and the one thing that is
    genuinely dangerous.

    --- VERIFIED SOUND ---

    * Exactly ONE place inserts into stored_files (the upload route). NOTHING
      DELETES FROM IT — there is no DELETE anywhere in the codebase — so a row
      can never be removed out from under a reference.

    * The hash is computed SERVER-SIDE from the bytes on disk, by streaming the
      temp file. It is never taken from the client, so a caller cannot claim a
      SHA that its content does not have.

    * storage_key IS the sha256, and the download route validates it as 64
      lowercase hex before building a path from it. Nothing client-supplied
      touches that path.

    * New content is created with linkSync, which FAILS if the name exists
      rather than overwriting. Two uploads of the same new content cannot
      silently produce two files.

    * The upload transaction is IMMEDIATE, so it takes the write lock at the
      start. better-sqlite3 is synchronous, so a second upload cannot interleave
      between the "is it in stored_files?" read and the insert.

    * Acceptance into the game store copies REFERENCES (INSERT ... SELECT from
      workspace_version_files), never the physical records. That is the
      mechanism behind the one-copy rule, and it is correct.

    * A row whose blob has gone missing is REPAIRED on the next upload of
      identical content, and a stray file at a new path is replaced rather than
      trusted.

    --- THE HOLE: NOTHING CHECKS THE DISK AGAINST THE DATABASE ---

    The database and the filesystem are two things and nothing enforced that
    they agree. A blob could be lost, or its contents corrupted, and the system
    would not notice until somebody tried to download it.

    check:store now does. `npm run check:store` verifies, in order of how much
    each matters: that every blob's bytes actually hash to the name it is stored
    under (content addressing intact — the one that matters most, since a file
    whose contents stopped matching its name means every reference serves the
    wrong thing); that no row is missing its blob; that no blob is without a
    row; and that the recorded file_size matches the disk. Read-only, so it is
    safe against a live database. --quick skips the re-hashing on a big store.

    IT FOUND ONE IMMEDIATELY, on its first run against the live store: a 6-byte
    orphan blob left by MY OWN cleanup after an upload test. The cleanup looked
    for a sharded path (uploads/<ab>/<sha>) and the store is FLAT
    (uploads/files/<sha>), so it deleted the row and left the file. Which is
    the whole argument for the check existing — that class of mistake is
    invisible without it, and I made it within a day.

    Suggested: run it before a backup and after any incident. Worth wiring into
    the admin page later (item 28) as the storage-health panel.

    --- STILL OPEN ---

    * THE RESET SCRIPTS ARE UNGUARDED — DECIDED 2026-10-02: REPLACE THEM WITH A
      DEVELOPMENT-MODE RESET OF THE ASSET STORE.

      The scripts do a real job — a clean store to test against — and they do it
      badly: backend/reset/resetAssetData.js deletes every asset table AND every
      file in the store, with NO confirmation, no dry run and no environment
      check. Its own comment records that a path bug once made it delete every
      stored_files row while reporting "nothing to delete", orphaning the
      physical files it had just untracked. That is the exact failure this item
      exists to prevent, and it has already happened once.

      The replacement is not a better script, it is THE SAME FACILITY AS ITEM 31
      behind a development-mode switch. It already has to:
        * be told what it is about to destroy, and agree to that specific list
        * refuse to run where it should not — and "where it should not" is now
          the point of the switch, not a path guess
        * leave an audit record

      SO THE DIFFERENCE FROM A NORMAL DELETE IS ONE THING: SCOPE, and the mode.
      Development mode resets the WHOLE store; a normal delete removes one
      asset. Both show what will go and both need agreement — the whole-store
      one just shows a great deal more.

      HARD RULE: IT MUST REFUSE TO RUN OUTSIDE DEVELOPMENT MODE. A production
      box that can be emptied by an admin clicking the wrong button is a worse
      failure than the scripts it replaces. Whatever the mode is read from — an
      explicit flag rather than NODE_ENV, so a mis-set environment cannot arm it
      — production must not be able to select it at all.

      AND IT MUST GO THROUGH THE SAME SAFETY AS GARBAGE COLLECTION: refuse to
      run if check:store reports the store inconsistent, because destroying a
      store you cannot trust is how a repairable problem becomes an unrepairable
      one. Note the symmetry — check:store tells you the store is sound,
      garbage collection removes what nothing references, and this removes
      everything. Only the last one is destructive by intent, and it is the one
      that most needs the other two to have run clean first.

    * A lost blob is only repaired by someone re-uploading identical content.
      check:store detects it; nothing repairs it. Acceptable while the store is
      small and rebuildable; worth revisiting before it is the only copy of
      anything that matters.


31) DELETING FROM THE GAME ASSET STORE — needed. NOTHING EXISTS.

    There is no note, spec, route or panel anywhere in the repo describing how
    to remove an approved asset from the store. That has been searched for
    explicitly, under every phrasing (delete, remove, withdraw, retract,
    unpublish, take down, purge), and the answer is nothing. The only mechanism
    in the repo that destroys store content is the unguarded reset script in
    item 30. So this is new ground rather than a continuation.

    THE NEED. Deleting from the store has to be possible, and most obviously
    during testing: the first assets pushed through the approval workflow will
    be junk, and today the only way to be rid of them is to wipe everything.
    Worth knowing: assets.is_active EXISTS ON THE TABLE AND IS READ BY NOTHING —
    the same dead-column state workspace_assets.asset_type was in. Soft delete
    was anticipated here and never wired up.

    THE REQUIREMENT: THE ADMIN IS TOLD WHAT IS BEING DELETED, AND AGREES.
    Not a dialog asking "are you sure?". The admin is shown what will actually
    go — the asset, its versions, its files, and what else references it — and
    agrees to that specific list. The same ask-and-confirm shape as the
    near-match prompt and the naming tiers: the server states the consequence,
    the human accepts it, the server acts. It belongs on the admin page with
    the rest of item 28.

    --- CONSTRAINTS THAT ALREADY EXIST AND WILL SHAPE IT ---

    * THE ONE-COPY RULE FORBIDS DELETING BLOBS. stored_files is shared, and a
      developer's workspace may still reference the same content. Deleting a
      store asset removes REFERENCES — asset_version_files rows — and never
      stored_files rows. It is then check:store's job to report any blob that
      nothing references any more.

    * THE DATABASE WILL REFUSE A NAIVE DELETE. There are no ON DELETE clauses
      anywhere, and an approved asset is referenced by asset_versions, by
      creative_approvals.repository_asset_id and repository_version_id, and by
      the same pair on asset_comments. Deletion is ordered and deliberate, or
      it fails.

    * docs/dbspec01 SAYS STORE ASSET VERSIONS ARE IMMUTABLE. That is a
      spec-level statement which deletion contradicts. It may well mean
      "immutable once released" rather than "immutable, full stop" — but that
      distinction has never been drawn, and this is where it has to be.

    * THE WORKSPACE ALREADY DOES THE OPPOSITE, ON PURPOSE. Its soft delete is
      documented as never touching the store tables, so an approved asset
      survives its author deleting their copy. That reasoning was about
      protecting history; deleting from the store has to be reconciled with it
      rather than quietly reversing it.

    --- DECIDED 2026-10-02: THEY ARE NOT THE SAME ACT ---

    WITHDRAWING SOMETHING THAT SHIPPED IS VERY EXTREME. It is a last resort,
    not a maintenance operation, and there should be LOTS OF BARS between the
    admin and the action — enough that the difficulty itself communicates how
    serious it is before anyone gets near it.

    REMOVING TEST DATA IS ORDINARY AND SHOULD BE NEARLY ONE STEP, especially
    when it is in the SANDBOX: nothing has been approved, nobody outside has
    seen it, and the only person affected is the developer who put it there.

    SO THE PANEL NEEDS TWO OPERATIONS OF DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT WEIGHT, not one
    delete with a severity setting. If they share a button and a confirmation
    dialog, that dialog becomes the only thing separating housekeeping from
    undoing a release — and no dialog can carry that distinction.

    --- THE SANDBOX HALF IS BUILT, AND IT FREES NOTHING ---

    Worth knowing before designing around it, because it is not what it looks
    like from the outside. A developer can already delete their own workspace
    content, and it is a SOFT delete: rows marked inactive, quota freed, and
    NOTHING REMOVED FROM DISK. And because nothing in the codebase ever deletes
    a stored_files row, the bytes stay forever — even after every reference to
    them is gone.

    So "just remove it from the sandbox" today is instant, safe, and frees no
    disk at all. For test data pushed through repeatedly that adds up, and it
    is invisible: the workspace shows empty while the store keeps every version
    of every abandoned experiment, charged to nobody.

    WHAT IS MISSING IS GARBAGE COLLECTION, not another delete button. A pass
    that finds stored_files rows referenced by NEITHER workspace_version_files
    NOR asset_version_files, and removes the row and the blob together.

    WHERE THE GARBAGE WOULD COME FROM: mostly the WASTEBIN PURGE — see the
    WASTEBIN section under WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE above. Delete puts
    the rows in the bin, the bin empties after 14 days and removes the file
    references, and this is what then frees the bytes. The two are one chain and
    are worth building together; a collector with nothing feeding it has nothing
    to collect.
    check:store already finds the mirror image of this — an orphan blob is the
    case where the FILE is present and the row is gone; this is the case where
    both are present and nothing points at either.

    SAFETY, because this is the one operation in the system that can destroy
    content outright:

      * It must run inside an IMMEDIATE transaction and RE-CHECK the references
        immediately before unlinking. A concurrent upload can find a
        stored_files row and be about to reference it; the check and the unlink
        cannot have a gap between them.

      * It should REFUSE TO RUN AT ALL if check:store reports the store
        inconsistent. Collecting garbage from a store you cannot trust is how a
        repairable problem becomes an unrepairable one.

      * Consider a grace period — only collect blobs older than, say, a day —
        so that a file written moments ago by an upload that is still in flight
        cannot be swept up by a pass running at the wrong moment.

    --- STILL OPEN, FOR THE STORE ITSELF ---

    * HARD OR SOFT — AND IT IS THE SAME ANSWER AS THE WORKSPACE. The same model
      applies, and it took the workspace note to see it: KEEP THE RECORD, DROP
      THE FILES. Releasing the store's asset_version_files rows is what frees
      the content; the collector frees the blob once nothing references it; and
      the asset, its versions, its approvals and its comments all stay readable.
      So it is not a choice between hard and soft — it is soft rows with the
      content released, exactly as in the workspace.

      (An earlier version of this note said that keeping the approval and
      comment history meant "the deletion is really a soft one and nothing is
      freed". That was wrong, and it was the same mistake made about the
      workspace: the RECORD is not what holds the bytes. The FILE ROWS are.)

    * SO WHAT DIFFERS IS FRICTION AND REACH, NOT MECHANISM. Withdrawing
      something that shipped is very extreme and wants lots of bars between the
      admin and it. Purging test data is ordinary and should be close to one
      step. Both end the same way — content released, record kept — and only the
      difficulty tells them apart. Two controls, deliberately different weight;
      see the DECIDED section above.

    * WHO — SETTLED 2026-10-02: ADMIN CAN DELETE. Not superuser-only. Deleting
      from the store is an admin capability, and superuser can do it too by the
      standing rule below. The superuser-only capabilities are the bigger,
      outward-facing ones: deploying the game, creating branches.

      THE ROLE RULE, and it is worth stating once in full because it is easy to
      break silently: SUPERUSER CAN DO EVERYTHING AN ADMIN CAN DO, plus more.
      A route naming 'admin' must name 'superuser' too. The failure mode is
      not an error — it is the most privileged account in the system being
      refused. Verified 2026-10-02: no requireRole call anywhere allows admin
      without superuser. The rule is now also written at requireRole() in
      backend/middleware/auth.js, which is where someone would look.

    --- AND THE TESTING CASE IS ALREADY HALF-BUILT, BADLY ---

    The reset scripts do this job today, unguarded — see STILL OPEN in item 30.
    A proper delete facility on the admin page may well be what replaces them:
    the same need, but with a confirmation, an audit trail, and no way to run
    it against the wrong database by accident.


32) SCHEDULER AND GARBAGE COLLECTION — confirmed needed 2026-10-02.

    NOTHING IN THIS SYSTEM RUNS ON A TIMER. Every operation so far is triggered
    by a request arriving. That was a simplification while nothing needed a
    clock; it is now a gap, because the wastebin has a 14-day window and the
    store has garbage that only collection will free.

    --- THE SCHEDULER ---

    WHAT IT HAS TO RUN, roughly in the order they start to matter:

      * the wastebin purge — see WASTEBIN under WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE
      * garbage collection — below
      * check:store, periodically, so a broken store is FOUND rather than
        discovered by somebody's download failing
      * later, whatever the admin page's thresholds need (item 28) — disk,
        memory and CPU are not much use sampled only when a page is loaded

    DESIGN QUESTIONS, none of them obvious:

      * WHAT MECHANISM. A setInterval in the server process is the least code
        and dies with the process. An external cron calling an authenticated
        endpoint survives restarts and leaves a record that it ran. node-cron
        sits in between. Given the eventual deployment is a self-hosted box,
        the external cron is probably the most honest: it does not depend on
        the app being up at the right moment, and a job that runs is
        distinguishable from a job that silently stopped.

      * MULTIPLE INSTANCES. An in-process timer runs the job once per process.
        Fine for an idempotent purge, wrong for anything that is not. Decide
        whether the jobs must be safe to run concurrently or whether something
        holds a lock — the answer probably differs per job.

      * WHAT HAPPENS WHEN A JOB FAILS. Silently is the wrong answer for a
        purge. A failed run should be visible — which means the admin page
        (item 28), and probably the audit log.

      * SHOULD IT ALSO RUN ON START? A box that was off for a month comes back
        with a month of backlog. Running once at startup is cheap, and stops the
        first request after a restart being the one that pays for it.

    --- GARBAGE COLLECTION ---

    Confirmed needed. It has TWO HALVES, and they are not equally ready:

      * ORPHAN BLOBS — a file on disk with no stored_files row. THIS ALREADY
        HAPPENS: it is exactly what check:store found on its first run, a 6-byte
        file left behind by an incomplete cleanup. Collectable today, and
        nothing else will ever remove it.

      * UNREFERENCED ROWS — a stored_files row whose content nothing points at,
        because every workspace_version_files and asset_version_files reference
        to it is gone. This is the one that frees real space and it cannot occur
        until the wastebin purge exists to create it.

    SAFETY, repeated from item 31 because it is the whole risk: re-check the
    references immediately before unlinking, inside the same IMMEDIATE
    transaction, with no gap for a concurrent upload to slip into; refuse to run
    at all if check:store reports the store inconsistent; and consider a grace
    period so a blob written moments ago by an in-flight upload cannot be swept
    up.

    AND IT SHOULD START REPORT-ONLY. A collector that deletes on its first
    scheduled run, having never once been watched, is how a store gets emptied
    by a bug. Run it, log what it WOULD remove, and turn deletion on once its
    output has been read a few times and matched what was expected.

    --- BUILD ORDER ---

    Two of the five are built, and they were the two everything else waited on:

      1. [DONE 2026-10-02] deleted_at on workspace_assets and
         workspace_versions, stamped on delete with COALESCE so re-deleting
         cannot restart the clock.
      2. [DONE 2026-10-02] Dropping the file rows: the file list is
         snapshotted as JSON first (name, path, size, hash — content never),
         then workspace_version_files goes. POST
         .../versions/:versionId/purge, and ONLY for versions already deleted,
         so the reversible step is never skipped by accident.
      3. [DONE 2026-10-02] The wastebin: a tab listing what is in the bin with
         a per-item countdown, and a restore for anything whose files are
         still there. A purged version is listed and marked unrestorable
         rather than hidden. The retention period is read from
         backend/lib/retention.js, where the scheduled purge will read it too.
      4. [DONE 2026-10-02] The scheduler: backend/jobs/daily.js, run from
         cron. One command, two jobs in order — the purge, then the
         collector. Documented in the manual with the cron line.
      5. [DONE 2026-10-02] Garbage collection, fed by the purge — and
         REPORT-ONLY by default, as decided. --collect turns deletion on.

    ALL FIVE ARE BUILT. What is left is not a step but a decision about when
    the machine actually starts doing this: the job must be IN CRON on the box
    before any of it happens on its own, and the collector should run plain for
    a while before anyone passes --collect.

    STILL NOT ADMIN-CONFIGURABLE. The retention period is a constant in
    backend/lib/retention.js. The decision was that an administrator changes it
    from the admin page, which needs the settings mechanism the schema still
    does not have (item 28). The value moves in ONE place when it exists —
    the bin and the job both read it — so nothing else has to change.

    NOTE WHAT STEP 2 DOES NOT DO, because the next step depends on it: it
    removes the REFERENCES, not the content. The blob is still on disk and will
    be until step 5 exists, and check:store reports the store as healthy
    throughout — correctly, because those rows DO still have their blobs. What
    is gone is the referrers, and finding those is the collector's job.

    THE FOREIGN KEY REBUILD THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE. It was only required
    if version and asset rows were going to be deleted, and they are not — the
    rows ARE the history being kept. Only the file rows go, and nothing
    references those. So the riskiest item in this plan is gone, and the design
    that removed it keeps MORE information than the one that needed it.

    Step 1 is small and unblocks everything. Steps 4 and 5 are the only
    genuinely new machinery; the rest is more of what already exists.


33) THE DEVELOPER'S ASSET HISTORY — BUILT 2026-10-02. One piece missing.

    All the assets a developer has worked on, including ones no longer live,
    each opening to show what happened to every version of it.

    WHY IT IS NOT THE LIST THAT ALREADY EXISTED. GET /assets filters to
    is_active = 1, which is right for what it is — the workspace a developer
    works IN right now — but it cannot show anything that has LEFT it. The
    moment a version was deleted or superseded, the record of what happened to
    it disappeared from sight. It was never lost; it had nowhere to be seen.

    BUILT:

      * GET /assets/history — every asset, live or not, every version, live or
        not, with the technical check result and the reason for a failure, the
        approval decision and the reviewer's comment, and whether the version
        is in the game store.

      * Navigation, where this dashboard previously had NONE: two tabs, Upload
        and History. History loads lazily on first open, because most visits
        are to upload rather than to look back.

      * The version renderer is the SAME ONE the workspace uses
        (renderVersionState), so a version reads identically in both views and
        there is no second implementation to drift. The endpoint's field names
        were changed to latest_check / latest_approval to match GET /assets for
        exactly that reason.

    VERIFIED, not assumed: an admin asking for history gets their OWN empty
    history and not the developer's, and an unauthenticated call is refused.
    That route is more tempting than most to open up, because it is read-only
    and looks harmless — but it is the developer's whole working record, so
    impersonation stays the only way in.

    STILL MISSING: THE DOWNLOAD — now a named stub. The developer dashboard has
    a "Download" entry beside Upload and History that reports itself as not
    built (frontend/src/notBuilt.js). The page also says plainly, on any version
    that is in the game store, that download is not built yet.

    It is the thing that makes "you can always download a copy" true rather than
    merely stated, and it is the same piece as item 25's import path. The
    manifest endpoint already exists and has never been called by anything; a
    per-file download is the wrong answer at fifty files, so this wants a ZIP.

    There is also a "Game store" stub, which is item 25 — browsing what is
    already approved rather than re-uploading it.

    AND THE PER-VERSION FILE RECORD IS NOT THERE YET, because nothing drops
    file rows yet. When files are dropped, the history should show what was
    uploaded from the record kept in their place — see THE WORKSPACE IS A
    STAGING AREA WITH A HISTORY.

    THE STAGING-AREA NOTICE is on the My Workspace card in the Upload tab, which
    is where a developer lands. If the navigation is ever reworked, keep it
    somewhere they actually arrive at.


34) FILE TYPE VERIFICATION — BUILT 2026-10-02. ONE function, one list.

    The asset-type field was taken off the upload page on the grounds that a
    developer should not be asked to restate what the file already says: Maya,
    Blender and Nuke are TOOLS, and the tool is a property of the file. That was
    done; the verifier that was supposed to replace it was NOT, and the plan had
    never been written down anywhere. Both halves are now done.

    ONE AND ONLY ONE FUNCTION. assetTypeOf(filename) in shared/assetRules.mjs.
    Nothing anywhere else classifies a file and nothing anywhere else keeps a
    list of extensions. It is the function the project asked for, in the place
    the project puts shared rules.

    THE LIST OF EXTENSIONS EXISTS ONCE, NOT TWICE. RESERVED_EXTENSIONS — the
    list answering "is this name a file or a folder?" — is now DERIVED from the
    type map, because both need the same vocabulary and two hand-kept copies of
    it would have drifted. One list, two questions. check:naming asserts the
    two agree, and that no extension appears under two types.

    WHERE IT RUNS: the technical check, as a real check with a pass/fail and a
    message, recorded like every other check. An UNRECOGNISED EXTENSION FAILS
    THE RUN rather than being shrugged at — it means nobody can say what the
    file is, and a check that ignores that is not checking anything.

    AND IT FILLS THE COLUMN, WHEN THERE IS ONE ANSWER. workspace_assets.
    asset_type is set from the check — but ONLY when every file in the version
    is the same type. An asset holding a .blend and forty .png textures has no
    single type, and reporting the most common would call it an Image asset when
    everyone would call it a Blender one. A confident wrong answer is worse than
    none, so it stays null and the per-file types are in the check message.
    COALESCE, so an ambiguous version cannot wipe a type an earlier one set.

    VERIFIED against a running server, three cases: one type sets the column
    ("Blender"); mixed content reports "Blender, Image" and leaves the column
    alone; an unknown extension fails the run and names the file.

    WHICH EXTENSIONS, AND WHERE THEY CAME FROM. 131 extensions across 21 types,
    drawn from what the engines and marketplaces actually accept rather than
    from a generic "file extensions" dump — Epic's Fab asset requirements and
    Unity's import list are the two most concrete published sources. Ordered
    GAME DEVELOPMENT FIRST: engines, then the tools that feed them, then the
    interchange and file formats around them.

    The order carries no behaviour; it is there so the common case sits at the
    top for whoever extends it next.

    DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, and it is a decision worth keeping: tracker audio
    (.mod .xm .it .s3m), CAD (.dwg), and legacy web 3D (.wrl .x3d). All real
    formats, none common in production — and every extension added is another
    name that stops being usable as an asset name. check:naming asserts the
    exclusions stay excluded, so a later bulk-import cannot quietly undo it.

    --- WHAT THE REAL CHECKS MUST GROW INTO ---

    The check runs as extension-only for now. Two more are wanted before these
    can be called the real checks, and both are about the same thing: believing
    a file's NAME instead of the file.

    * NO FILE IS EMPTY — BUILT 2026-10-02. A zero-byte file passes every other
      check here: it exists, its size matches what was recorded, its path is
      unique, and its extension is recognised. And it is never a real asset —
      it is a failed export, an interrupted write, or a placeholder somebody
      meant to come back to. Verified: an empty .blend passed files_present,
      the per-file check AND the type check, and failed only this one.

    * THE CONTENTS MUST MATCH THE EXTENSION — BUILT 2026-10-02. The extension
      is a CLAIM; this is what turns it into something a reviewer can rely on,
      or says plainly that it cannot.

      FOUR OUTCOMES, NOT TWO, and the fourth is the point:

        confirmed      the bytes match a signature this extension may have
        contradicted   they match something ELSE — the name is wrong, or the
                       file is not what it claims
        blocked        refused content, whatever it is called
        unverifiable   nothing reliable to check against

      UNVERIFIABLE IS NOT A PASS. A file the system cannot vouch for is
      reported as such rather than counted as good. A tick that cannot fail
      tells a reviewer nothing, and telling them nothing while looking like
      approval is worse than saying so. The count is reported ALWAYS, including
      when something failed — dropping it on failure would hide how much of an
      upload was never actually checked.

      BLOCKED IS SEPARATE FROM WRONG, as two checks: a .png that is really a
      JPEG is a mislabelled asset, a .png that is really a Windows executable is
      not an asset at all. Burying both in one list would hide the one that
      matters. Executable signatures are checked BEFORE the extension, so a
      rename cannot get past it.

      WHAT IS NOT BLOCKED, deliberately: anything identified by "#!" or a text
      prefix. Shell and Python scripts share those markers with legitimate files
      here — a Nuke script and a Maya script are text too — so a rule built on
      them would fail real assets. Running uploaded code is prevented by nothing
      ever executing a stored file (item 35), not by this list.

      SIGNS 61 OF 131 EXTENSIONS. The rest are text formats with no reliable
      opening bytes — .obj, .ma, .nk, .dae, .gltf — and they report as
      unverifiable. A format is listed ONLY if a file of that format ALWAYS
      starts with those bytes; anything less would be a check that passes by
      accident. Structural checks (a .usda must open with #usda, an .obj has
      v/f line forms) would cover some of the gap and are a separate job.

      LIVES BESIDE assetTypeOf() in shared/assetRules.mjs. The type map and the
      signature table describe the same vocabulary, and kept apart they would
      drift — check:naming now asserts that no signature exists for an
      extension the type map has never heard of.

      VERIFIED end to end: a real PNG confirmed; a JPEG named .png contradicted
      as "looks like JPEG, not PNG"; a Windows executable named .png BLOCKED,
      with the run failed; a Nuke script reported as one of the files that could
      not be verified.


35) NOTHING UPLOADED EVER RUNS. A standing rule, not a task.

    ONLY THE FUNCTIONS WE PROVIDE MAY RUN IN A WORKSPACE. A developer uploads
    files; those files are DATA. They are never executed, never evaluated, never
    interpreted, and never served in a way that lets a browser run them. This is
    the rule the whole workspace rests on, and it is worth writing down because
    the ways to break it are all convenient.

    VERIFIED 2026-10-02, because a rule nobody has checked is a hope:

      * THE UPLOAD STORE IS NOT SERVED STATICALLY. express.static is mounted on
        frontend/dist and frontend/public only. The content directory
        (backend/uploads/files) has no route that maps onto it — a request
        cannot name a path inside it.

      * NOTHING IN THE BACKEND EXECUTES ANYTHING. No child_process, no exec, no
        eval, no new Function, no vm. Verified by searching the whole backend.
        The technical check READS files to hash and size them; that is the only
        thing anything does with a stored file.

      * CONTENT IS SERVED AS application/octet-stream WITH
        Content-Disposition: attachment AND X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff,
        and NEVER with the stored mime_type. The mime_type is there for display
        and is never a response header. Serving a developer-supplied .svg or
        .html inline from this origin would be stored XSS against whoever
        opened it, which is a worse version of the same mistake.

      * The Content-Disposition filename is built from a developer-supplied
        name, so quotes and backslashes are stripped and non-ASCII is escaped.
        A filename is not allowed to break out of its own header.

    WHAT THAT MEANS FOR ANYTHING BUILT LATER, and these are the ones that will
    be tempting:

      * NO PREVIEW THAT INTERPRETS. An image shown inline is a decision to
        re-examine, not a detail — and anything that renders SVG, HTML or a
        model in a way that can run script has broken this rule outright.

      * NO SERVER-SIDE CONVERSION. A thumbnailer, a video transcoder, an
        archive extractor: each one opens a file we did not write with a parser
        we did not write. That is the definition of the risk this rule exists to
        prevent, and it deserves its own decision rather than being added as a
        convenience.

      * NO ARCHIVE EXTRACTION without solving zip-slip first — an entry named
        ../../ is how an extractor writes outside the directory it was given.

      * NO CONFIG OR SCRIPT A DEVELOPER UPLOADS EVER RUNS. Not an .sh, not a
        .json a tool would read as configuration, not a build file. If we ever
        run a developer's build, that is a decision with a sandbox attached, not
        a feature.
